{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-recovery", "file": "references/rectangle-fill-selection.md", "content": "## Rectangle interior fills: infer selection separately from filling

A rejected task-1a8d9993 test submission exposed a key ambiguity: the operation of filling a selected region with color 4 was plausible, but applying it to every apparent rectangle was incorrect. The demo input contained several visually region-like shapes; its output changed one isolated interior cell and the 4×4 interior of a clear lower-right frame, while leaving other apparent candidates unchanged. Thus “strict interior → 4” is only the conditional action, not the full rule. Determine which region/object qualifies from cross-demo evidence, including unchanged candidate regions. After rejection, do not repeat the same fill-all interpretation or speculate a broader geometry; spend a demo only if it can discriminate the selector. Preserve boundaries/exteriors and validate dimensions after identifying qualifying regions.

The task-specific skill `arc-task-1a8d9993` contains historical interior-fill geometry notes but is user-owned; this general recovery principle is stored here in the curator-managed umbrella.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u036/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-demo-recovery/references/rectangle-fill-selection.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-synthesis", "file": "references/rejected-submissions-and-review.md", "content": "# Rejected ARC submissions and turn discipline

After a wrong answer, discard the candidate's whole rule, not merely its selected color or endpoint. Explicitly contrast its test prediction with all complete demonstrations, including unchanged lookalike cases, and change course only for an interpretation supported by that evidence. A depleted demo budget is not evidence that the remaining guess is correct. Keep output actions grounded in full demo replay rather than using repeated near-duplicate submissions to probe the grader.

In long demonstration streams, repeated skill/reference loading is not analysis: load applicable guidance once, process each newly returned pair against earlier ones, then choose one action. In protocol-constrained ARC, the response should be exactly one action JSON object.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u036/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-demo-synthesis/references/rejected-submissions-and-review.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-evidence-processing", "file": "references/task-4431168d-contradictory-parity-session.md", "content": "# task-4431168d — conflicting frame-fill evidence

One demo pair showed a 3×3 color-3 hollow square and a 3×3 color-5 hollow square; each background center became 7. A 6×6 color-6 frame's interior became 2. These observations fit odd-square→7 and even-square→2, but are not sufficient to establish a general rule.

The test grid contained multiple apparent frames, including odd squares, even squares, mixed-dimension rectangles, and outlines embedded alongside other shapes. Two submissions applying substantially the same parity-based fill hypothesis were rejected. This demonstrates that the tentative parity rule or the identification/eligibility of the frames was incomplete. Do not repeat the same candidate or merely tweak a color. On recurrence, record each complete frame's bounding box, dimensions, perimeter color, strict-interior contents, and exact changed cells from demos; use another demo to separate dimension mapping from selection criteria before submitting.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u036/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-demo-evidence-processing/references/task-4431168d-contradictory-parity-session.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-synthesis", "file": "references/demo-synthesis-serial-request-lesson.md", "content": "# Demo integration before requesting again

In a session with several costly demos, the main operational failure was requesting the next example immediately after receiving the previous one, without stating its constraint or comparing it to the live hypotheses. The later examples were rich and heterogeneous; proper synthesis should have extracted their changed/unchanged cells and cross-compared them before another request.

For every returned pair, write down what it proves and what remains ambiguous. Request another only when you can name the competing rules and how the possible pair outcomes would change the test prediction. Do not treat request availability or uncertainty as sufficient justification. This complements the general demo synthesis workflow; it does not establish a transformation rule for this task, whose demos in this conversation did not resolve the input motif.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u036/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-demo-synthesis/references/demo-synthesis-serial-request-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-demo-synthesis", "file": "references/sparse-pattern-completion-lesson.md", "content": "# Selective sparse-pattern completion: unresolved row-selection rule

This session supplied eight heterogeneous demonstrations for a task whose test was a 29×24 grid with sparse 7-patterns on background 5. In several demonstrations, a few cells at the right ends of selected rows/regions changed from background to 1. Examples included right-edge row tails (2 or 4 cells, depending on grid width), a small 2×2 corner patch, and other selective edge blocks. Many visually similar rows and repeated-looking counterparts remained untouched. Full-width bands were often unchanged except for a single anomalous cell, which then became 1. The final pair reinforced that tail extension occurs only for selected patterns, not every non-background row.

Two test submissions were wrong: first the input was preserved, then alternating background cells in a row were recolored. Neither demonstrates a validated rule. The selection predicate remains unresolved. For future instances, classify this as sparse-pattern completion/repair, but do not fill all row tails, all repeated rows, or a broad rectangle. Transcribe all changed and unchanged cells from each pair, form competing explicit selection hypotheses, and reproduce every demo cellwise before testing. If the demo cap is reached without a rule, submit only the best-supported candidate and never label it validated.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u036/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-demo-synthesis/references/sparse-pattern-completion-lesson.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-panel-mask-validation", "file": "references/aligned-panel-overlap.md", "content": "# Aligned two-panel overlap masks

In separator-split grids with two same-sized panels and output the size of one panel, do not assume the output copies one side. Test foreground-mask relations at each aligned coordinate: intersection, union, and directional differences. The decisive cells are those where exactly one panel is foreground. If output foreground appears only where both panels are foreground across every demo, the rule is an intersection mask; map its output palette separately. Mechanically replay every cell and every demo before applying to the test.

In task-06d696f4, three demos used differing dimensions and colors. In each, output was the cellwise intersection of the top and bottom panel foreground masks, with output foreground recolored to 2 and background to 0. Single-panel-only markers were suppressed. The test prediction should therefore be 2 exactly where the upper panel has 1 and the lower panel has 9; elsewhere 0.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/USER/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/stage1/HA-hermes-luna6-low-rnd0/frozen/seen/play/users/u036/system/state/hermes/skills/arc/arc-panel-mask-validation/references/aligned-panel-overlap.md"}
